<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s07e06" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 6: Integer Exponents and Exponent Properties</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 6 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use exponent properties to simplify expressions
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    Remember that when we simplified a product like
    <md>
      x^3\cdot x^4
    </md>
    we remembered that exponents indicated repeated multiplication:
    <md>
      x\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x\cdot x
    </md>
    We observed that we had a total of 7 multiplied x’s, giving us an answer:
    <md>
      x^7
    </md>
    Notice that we ended up adding the exponents together.
    <md>
      x^3 \cdot x^4 = x^{3+4}
    </md>
  </p>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Exponent Property One</title>
    <p>
      Let <m>m</m> and <m>n</m> stand for two numbers (that may be different from problem to problem). 
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have the base <m>x</m> raised to a power, and then you multiply that by the same base <m>x</m> raised to a different power, 
      you should add the two exponents together.
      <md>
        x^m \cdot x^n = x^{n+m}
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <p>
      Simplify:
      <md>
        x^{100} \cdot x^{214}
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      It would be tedious to write out 100 x’s and 214 x’s, so instead, we’ll use our exponent property. It tells us we should add the exponents together:
      <md>
        x^{100} \cdot x^{214} = x^{100+214} = x^{214}
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Now let’s think about what happens when we raise something to a power, and then raise that result to another power, like:
    <md>
      (x^3)^4
    </md>
    Remember that an exponent of 4 means multiplying 4 copies of the base together:
    <md>
      x^3 \cdot x^3 \cdot x^3 \cdot x^3
    </md>
    And each <m>x^{3}</m> is the same as 3 copies of <m>x</m>:
    <md>
      x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x
    </md>
    In total, we see we have 12 copies of x multiplied together:
    <md>
      x^{12}
    </md>
    Notice that we ended up multiplying together the exponents. 
    <md>
      (x^3)^4 = x^{3\cdot 4}
    </md>
  </p>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Exponent Property Two</title>
    <p>
      Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> stand for two numbers (that may be different from problem to problem). 
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have the base <m>x</m> raised to a power, and then you raise that result to another power, you should multiply the two exponents together.
      <md>
        (x^a)^b = x^{a\cdot b}
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <p>
      Simplify:
      <md>
        (x^{20})^4
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: It would be tedious to write out 20 copies of x, so let’s use our exponent property. It says we should multiply the exponents:
      <md>
        (x^{20})^4 = x^{20\cdot 4} = x^{80}
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Now let's think about what happens when we multiply two things together, and then raise that result to a power, like:
    <md>
      (xy)^3
    </md>
    This means we should multiply 3 copies of the base together:
    <md>
      x\cdot y \cdot x\cdot y \cdot x\cdot y
    </md>
    All together, we have 3 copies of x and 3 copies of y:
    <md>
      x^3 y^3
    </md>
    Notice that we ended up raising x to the power 3 and separately raising y to the power 3.
  </p>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Exponent Property Three</title>
    <p>
      Let <m>p</m> stand for a number (that may be different from problem to problem). 
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have a product in your base, and you raise that product to a power, you can apply that power to each piece of the product separately:
      <md>
        (xy)^p = x^p y^p
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <p>
      Simplify:
      <md>
        (2x)^5
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: It would be tedious to write out 5 copies of <m>2x</m>, so we use our exponent properties. 
      <md>
        (2x)^5 = 2^5 x^5  = 32x^5
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Be Careful!</title>
    <p>
      When you apply an exponent to a product, you can apply the exponent to each part of the product:
      <md>
        (3x)^2 = 3^2x^2
      </md>
      This is the most common mistake that I (Michael) make, forgetting to square the number!
    </p>
    <p>
      When you apply an exponent to a sum or different, you CANNOT apply the exponent to each part of the sum. Instead, you should distribute:
      <md>
        <mrow>(x+3)^2 \amp = (x+3)(x+3) </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = x^2+3x+3x+9 </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = x^2+6x+9 \neq x^2 + 3^2</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s07e06">
    <title>Season 7, Episode 6 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Use the exponent properties to simplify the following.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>x^{12}\cdot x^{87}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>z^{200}\cdot z^{57}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>b^{150}\cdot b^{248}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y^{35}\cdot y^{164}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>a^{3}\cdot x^{399}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>m^{78}\cdot m^{121}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Use the exponent properties to simplify the following.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(x^{12})^7</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(z^{20})^{15}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(b^{17})^{23}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(y^5)^{32}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(a^{3})^{50}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.8in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(m^8)^{49}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section> 